Mesh and rig the **Aegis**, a colossal multi-gun walking fortress that strides on
six independent legs — each a three-segment thigh/shin/flat-foot chain — whose main
cannon aims forward, whose two side-mounted turrets each traverse to cover only their
own flank, and whose radar vane sweeps on its own, as an 88x80x104 **Dual Contouring**
mesh using only the `dc-anim` tool. Dual Contouring preserves **sharp edges and
corners**, so build the fortress as **crisp hard-surface armor** — clean edges,
defined panel seams — not a rounded, smoothed shape. You author its walk and weapon
animations as F-curves.

## How meshing and rigging works

The only way to shape the surface and edit the rig is the `dc-anim` binary already
on your `PATH`. It is the sole channel: unlike a voxel tool it does not paint cells —
it maintains a **continuous signed-distance field** you shape by **compositing CSG
primitives** (add/subtract spheres, boxes, ellipsoids, cylinders), and **Dual
Contouring** extracts a crisp triangle mesh from that field's zero level set. You
build the model by calling the tool **one operation at a time**, and the ordered
operations you issue — recorded per part — together with the rig you define are the
**authoritative output that is scored**, not any file you write directly. Anything
produced another way is discarded and flagged; only the recorded operations and the
rig count.

Every operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is an
**independently-authored field** with its own preview and its own emitted mesh. The
field starts **empty**. Primitives carry **opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no
transparency — and their centers and extents are **real-valued** within the volume
(not snapped to a grid). After each call the binary extracts that part's mesh and
re-renders its wgpu preview to `parts/<part>.png`, and emits that part's geometry to
`parts/<part>.mesh.json`. Blank starting previews, empty per-part logs, and a
pre-seeded `rig.json` holding the required parts, joints, and animation declarations
are already in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
dc-anim add-box --part chassis --x 44 --y 44 --z 52 --width 60 --height 30 --depth 84 --color "#c69a4b" --sharp
```

Run `dc-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining
parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and defining animations and their keyframes),
and `dc-anim <operation> --help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is
the authoritative contract. Note the field vocabulary: additive `add-sphere` /
`add-box` / `add-ellipsoid` / `add-cylinder`, subtractive `subtract-*` to carve,
`--blend <radius>` for a smooth (rounded) join versus a hard crease, and the
Dual-Contouring-only **`--sharp` / `--smooth`** tag that controls whether an edge is
preserved crisply or rounded. **Read `parts/<part>.png` after your calls** to see
that part's surface take shape, and judge how the whole fortress fits together from
the assembled 3D view the parts compose into — that the six legs seat under the hull
and reach the ground, the main turret sits centered on the citadel, the cannon meets
the turret front, and each side turret seats out on its sponson. Because each part is
authored in the shared volume's coordinates, position each part's field where it
belongs on the finished fortress.

